Description: tuning for hosts running many desktop sessions at once
 Settings for an LTSP application server: a host where a lot of people are
 logged into graphical sessions simultaneously, over RDP, with their home
 directories on a file server.
 .
 It does three things. It moves each session's cache off the network home
 directory onto local disk, because ~/.cache is the busiest directory a
 desktop has and none of it is worth sending over NFS. It turns off window
 manager compositing by default, which on a session server without a GPU is
 done by the CPU once per session and buys nothing over a compressed remote
 desktop stream. And it raises the inotify limits a full Xfce session spends
 freely, whose exhaustion otherwise shows up as a misleading "too many open
 files".
 .
 The settings are deliberately few. README.Debian lists what was left out
 after being measured as already correct on Debian 13, so the next person
 does not spend an afternoon re-adding it.

